---@class posthog.messaging.CreateMessagingPreferenceRequest
---@field project_id string Project ID of the project you're trying to access. To find the ID of the project, make a call to /api/projects/.
---@field identifier string The recipient's email address, exactly as Workflows sends to it. Matching is exact, so `Jane@example.com` and `jane@example.com` are two recipients.
---@field all_marketing? boolean `false` opts the recipient out of every marketing topic. `true` lifts that opt-out. Applied before `topics`.
---@field topics? posthog.messaging.CreateMessagingPreferenceRequestTopicsMap Topic key to `false` to opt the recipient out of that topic, or `true` to opt them back in. Opting back in to one marketing topic after an all-marketing opt-out lifts that opt-out, so `all_marketing` becomes `true`, and keeps the other marketing topics opted out. Topics you leave out keep their current choice.

---Topic key to `false` to opt the recipient out of that topic, or `true` to opt them back in. Opting back in to one marketing topic after an all-marketing opt-out lifts that opt-out, so `all_marketing` becomes `true`, and keeps the other marketing topics opted out. Topics you leave out keep their current choice.
---@alias posthog.messaging.CreateMessagingPreferenceRequestTopicsMap { [string]: boolean }

---@class posthog.messaging.EmailAttachment
---@field filename string File name the recipient sees, such as `invoice.pdf`. No path and no line breaks. A file type mailbox providers block, such as `.exe`, is refused.
---@field content string The file's bytes as standard base64, without line breaks.
---@field content_type? string MIME type of the file, such as `application/pdf`. Without it, the type follows from the file name.
---@field content_id? string Content ID that the HTML body references as `cid:<content_id>` to show the file inline, such as `logo`. Without `<` or `>`.

---* `queued` - Queued
---* `failed` - Failed
---* `sent` - Sent
---* `delivered` - Delivered
---* `bounced` - Bounced
---* `complained` - Complained
---@alias posthog.messaging.EmailDeliveryStatusEnum "queued"|"failed"|"sent"|"delivered"|"bounced"|"complained"

---@class posthog.messaging.EmailStatus
---@field id string The `message_id` that the send returned.
---@field status posthog.messaging.EmailDeliveryStatusEnum What happened to the email. `queued` means it waits to go out. `sent` means PostHog handed it to the email provider. `failed` means it was not sent, and `reason` says why. A rerun of the send can still move it to `sent`. `delivered` means the recipient's mail server accepted it. `bounced` and `complained` come later from the recipient's mail provider. * `queued` - Queued * `failed` - Failed * `sent` - Sent * `delivered` - Delivered * `bounced` - Bounced * `complained` - Complained
---@field created_at string When the send was queued.
---@field status_at string When the email reached its current status.
---@field reason string|distilled.Null Why a failed email was not sent, or the bounce type of a bounced email. A failed send has `provider_error`, or `provider_rejected` when the provider refused the email after it accepted the send. A skipped send has `recipient_suppressed`, `recipient_unreachable`, `recipient_opted_out`, `missing_recipient`, `sending_paused`, `sending_suspended` or `frequency_capped`. A run that stopped before the send has `canceled`, `workflow_unavailable` or an error kind such as `timeout`, and more error kinds can be added. A bounce has `hard`, `soft` or `unknown`. Null otherwise.

